# Bentley Mark VI standard steel saloon, 1946-1952.
#
# The first post-war Bentley and the first Rolls-Royce/Bentley sold as a complete
# factory-bodied car: the "standard steel" saloon designed by Ivan Evernden (J.P.
# Blatchley at Crewe), built by Pressed Steel and assembled at Crewe. 4,257 cc
# six, 132 bhp, four-speed manual, independent front suspension; 93 mph. 5,208
# built 1946-52, of which 3,171 were standard steel saloons; coachbuilders made
# the rest. Replaced by the R-Type.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bentley_Mark_VI
# https://www.bentleymedia.com/en/heritage-collection/1949-mark-vi-ago2
#
# Dimensions: 4,877 mm long, 1,778 mm wide, 1,638 mm high, wheelbase 3,048 mm.
#
# Colours: black was the usual finish; maroon, dark blue and grey were standard
# alternatives. The photographed car is a black-and-grey two-tone; solid versions
# of the same colours are modelled.
#
# Weight: 5,208 cars over six years, the most successful Bentley to that date but
# still a rare sight; set at 8 in total (black 3, maroon 2, blue 2, grey 1).
# Provisional pending the central rebase.
#
# Speed 130 km/h: a game-balance figure; the period maximum was 93 mph.
# Comfort 160/420: a softly sprung, well-trimmed post-war luxury saloon, above
# the pre-war 20/25's 150/405; the line gives 420.
#
# Dates describe road presence: 1946 to the mid-1960s.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/bentley-mk6.blend in the blends repository.
# References: citycars/references/bentley-mk6/ (gitignored).
# Image credits: 1948, 1950 and 1951 Mark VI photographs via Wikimedia Commons
# (see reference file names for the photographers and licences).

